        "The supplied abbreviation tables show why context matters: LOC can mean level of consciousness or loss of consciousness; CO can mean cardiac output or carbon monoxide; BS can refer to blood sugar, breath sounds, or bowel sounds. A short form is not automatically clear because it is in a medical table. Use abbreviations your system accepts and spell out a term when the intended meaning could be misunderstood. In verbal communication, plain language can prevent a mistaken interpretation. The audiobook also distinguishes abbreviations, initialisms, and acronyms, while warning that shortened drug names can be unsafe. Nitro, MS, and MSO4 should not substitute for a full medication name in an order or report.",
        "Compare 'LOC changed' with an actual description of responsiveness and when it changed. The second preserves the clinical fact. A reader should not need to guess whether your note means the patient became less alert or actually lost consciousness. Never invent a new abbreviation just because it is shorter. Write or say the full medication name, dose, and units, and use the locally approved abbreviation list. Ask for clarification rather than silently choosing an expansion."
